rnalID":"s:21IncidenceMatrixModule08OrientedaB0VyACxc14GraphProtocols08DirectedE0RzAD0G10HypergraphRzlufc","symbolKind":"init","roleHeading":"Initializer","fragments":[{"text":"init","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"H","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">(","kind":"text"},{"text":"H","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":")","kind":"text"}],"modules":[{"name":"IncidenceMatrixModule"}]},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix\/init(_:)-y850"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix","abstract":[{"text":"The oriented incidence matrix of a directed hypergraph: entry (v, p) is −1 when ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"text":" is a source","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of column ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"p"},{"text":", +1 when it is a target, and 0 otherwise. Gallo, Longo, Pallottino and Nguyen","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(1993) define a directed hypergraph’s matrix this way (aᵢⱼ = −1 for a tail node, 1 for a head","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"node); a directed graph’s edge is the column with one source and one target, the matrix of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"spectral graph theory (B·Bᵀ = L, Godsil–Royle §8.3) and NetworkX’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidence_matrix(oriented=True)"},{"text":". It conforms to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedHypergraph"},{"text":", so the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"algorithms (B- and F-connection, components, the clique expansion) run on it unchanged; it is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"not a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix","title":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedGraph":{"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"DirectedGraph","role":"symbol","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"DirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedgraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A directed graph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of directed edges between"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"them."}]},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix/init(_:)-y850":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix\/init(_:)-y850","title":"init(_:)","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The one hypergraph conversion for a type that is both a directed graph and a directed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"hypergraph: read as a hypergraph."}],"url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix\/init(_:)-y850","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"init","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"H","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">(","kind":"text"},{"text":"H","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":")","kind":"text"}]},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule":{"abstract":[],"kind":"symbol","title":"IncidenceMatrixModule","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule","role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"DirectedHypergraph","role":"symbol","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedhypergraph","abstract":[{"text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","type":"text"}]},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop (XGI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DiHypergraph"},{"text":" allows both).","type":"text"}]}}}